// Wonder Lab earth-science data. Generated by tools/build_extras.py
// Written and fact-checked in review passes; do not hand-edit.
// 97 facts across 8 sections.
const EARTH = [{"id":"weather-1","section":"weather","cat":"record","kind":"found","text":"A lightning bolt heats the air in its path to about 50,000 F (27,800 C). The surface of the sun is around 10,000 F (5,500 C). So the air inside a lightning channel is roughly five times hotter than the surface of the sun, for about a ten-thousandth of a second.","more":"It is not that the bolt is hot to begin with. Lightning is a huge current of electricity forcing its way through air, which is a terrible conductor. The air fights it, and that resistance is what dumps the heat, the same way a thin wire glows orange inside a toaster."},{"id":"weather-10","section":"weather","cat":"size","kind":"found","text":"An ordinary puffy cumulus cloud carries around 1.1 million pounds (500,000 kg) of water. That is a loaded jumbo jet, parked in the sky. It stays up because the water is chopped into droplets far too small to fall quickly, and the air underneath the cloud is denser than the cloud is.","more":"The droplets are falling, just extremely slowly. Rising air underneath keeps shoving them back up. Rain starts when droplets bump into each other and merge. Once they are heavy enough, they win the argument."},{"id":"weather-11","section":"weather","cat":"speed","kind":"found","text":"The fastest wind ever measured in a tornado was read by a radar mounted on a truck near Bridge Creek, Oklahoma on 3 May 1999. The first figure was about 301 mph (484 km/h). A careful re-check in 2021 raised it to 321 mph (517 km/h).","more":"The radar was looking at wind a few hundred feet up, not at ground level. No weather instrument sitting on the ground survives a tornado core. So tornado strength is normally rated afterward, from what the damage looks like."},{"id":"weather-12","section":"weather","cat":"created","kind":"record","text":"On day two the waters were divided, water below from water above. The water above is overhead right now. One ordinary afternoon cloud carries a million pounds of it.","more":"That a cloud holds about a million pounds of water is weighed and calculated. Where the water above came from is read out of Genesis. Those are two different kinds of statement."},{"id":"weather-2","section":"weather","cat":"tryit","kind":"found","text":"Thunder is the sound of air superheated by a lightning bolt blowing outward. Light gets to you almost instantly. Sound crawls, at about one mile every five seconds. So count from the flash to the bang, divide by five, and that is roughly how many miles off the strike was.","more":"Thunder carries about ten miles before the sound gives out. If you can hear it at all, you are close enough to be hit, which is why the safety rule is: when thunder roars, go indoors.","tryit":"From inside the house, count one-Mississippi, two-Mississippi from the next flash until the thunder arrives. Divide by five for miles. Do it twice and you can tell whether the storm is walking toward you or away."},{"id":"weather-3","section":"weather","cat":"record","kind":"found","text":"The biggest hailstone ever measured in the United States fell on Vivian, South Dakota on 23 July 2010. It was 8 inches (20.3 cm) across, 18.62 inches (47.3 cm) around the middle, and weighed 1.94 pounds (0.88 kg). Roughly a soccer ball made of ice.","more":"It sat in a home freezer before the National Weather Service could get to it, and had already shrunk a little by the time it was measured. So the stone that actually landed was bigger than the record it set."},{"id":"weather-4","section":"weather","cat":"weird","kind":"worked","text":"Cut a big hailstone open and you find layers, like an onion. The old explanation, one layer per trip up and down inside the storm, is wrong. Nobody can follow a hailstone around inside a thundercloud. The layers are read as patches of different temperature and wetness it drifted through.","more":"Clear layers form where the stone is collecting water fast enough that it freezes slowly and squeezes the air bubbles out. Cloudy white layers form where it freezes on contact and traps the bubbles. Most of the growing happens in one pass across the updraft, not in loops."},{"id":"weather-5","section":"weather","cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"Wilson Bentley bolted a camera to a microscope and worked outdoors in the cold. On 15 January 1885 he made the first photograph of a single snow crystal. He was nineteen, on a farm in Jericho, Vermont. It had taken him two winters of failures. He kept going for the rest of his life.","more":"He photographed more than 5,000 crystals. His book Snow Crystals came out in 1931 with 2,453 of the pictures in it, and he died that same year, of pneumonia caught walking home in a snowstorm. Plenty of the snowflake pictures in schoolbooks are still his."},{"id":"weather-6","section":"weather","cat":"weird","kind":"worked","text":"Nobody has ever checked whether no two snowflakes are alike. Nobody has compared them all. Here is what can be said. A large crystal is built from something like a billion billion water molecules. Each of its six arms grows through its own path of cold and damp. Small plain crystals do look identical.","more":"There is a narrower claim people can actually defend. Two big, complicated crystals matching arm for arm would be a staggering coincidence. Two simple six-sided plates matching is ordinary. That has been photographed."},{"id":"weather-7","section":"weather","cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"Snow crystals have six sides because ice does. When water freezes, the molecules lock into a repeating six-sided pattern, and the crystal grows outward on that frame. The six arms end up matching each other because they are growing side by side through the same air, not because anything is copying anything."},{"id":"weather-8","section":"weather","cat":"record","kind":"found","text":"The hottest air temperature ever officially recorded is 134 F (56.7 C). It was read at Greenland Ranch in Death Valley, California, on 10 July 1913. It is still the world record. It is also still argued about. Some researchers argue the observer misread the thermometer that week.","more":"Death Valley regularly gets past 125 F (52 C) in modern, well-checked instruments, so the place is not in doubt. Only that one reading is."},{"id":"weather-9","section":"weather","cat":"record","kind":"found","text":"The coldest air temperature ever recorded is minus 128.6 F (minus 89.2 C). It was read at the Soviet Vostok station in Antarctica on 21 July 1983. July is midwinter down there. Throw a cup of boiling water into the air at that temperature. It can turn to ice crystals before it lands."},{"id":"quakes-1","section":"quakes","cat":"record","kind":"found","text":"Each whole step up the magnitude scale means the ground shakes ten times as far, and about 32 times as much energy comes out. So a magnitude 7 is not a bit worse than a 6. It would take roughly 32 sixes to add up to one seven.","more":"Two steps, from 6 to 8, is a hundred times the shaking and about a thousand times the energy. The scale is written that way because otherwise the numbers would run into the millions."},{"id":"quakes-10","section":"quakes","cat":"tryit","kind":"found","text":"One earthquake sends out two kinds of wave from the same spot at the same moment. The push-pull P wave travels fastest through rock and arrives first. The sideways S wave lags behind and does more damage. Measure the gap between them and you know how far away the quake was.","tryit":"Press your ear flat on a wooden table and have someone tap the far end. You hear the tap through the wood before you hear it through the air. Same idea: how fast a shake travels depends entirely on what it is traveling through."},{"id":"quakes-11","section":"quakes","cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"Some buildings do not sit on the ground. They sit on thick pads of rubber layered with steel and lead. When the ground jerks sideways, the pads squash and shear and the building above lags behind, moving far less than the dirt under it.","more":"Hospitals, museums and emergency centers are built this way in Japan, New Zealand and California. Instruments inside them have recorded real earthquakes. The buildings did what the design said they would."},{"id":"quakes-12","section":"quakes","cat":"created","kind":"record","text":"Day three in Genesis is land moving on a scale nobody has ever watched. The waters under the sky are gathered into one place, and dry land appears. The account gives an order, water first and then land standing out of it, and calls the dry part Earth.","more":"Genesis names the dry land on day three, gathered out from under the water. Water first, then land standing out of it. Hold that order in mind at a cliff face of flat layers. Those layers were laid down wet."},{"id":"quakes-2","section":"quakes","cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"Charles Richter built his scale in 1935 for medium earthquakes in southern California. The name then stuck to everything after it. Big quakes broke the scale. Above about magnitude 7 it flattens out and stops telling them apart. The numbers you hear today come from the moment magnitude scale instead.","more":"News reports still say Richter out of habit. The two scales are set up to agree in the middle of the range, so a modern magnitude 5 means about what Richter's magnitude 5 meant."},{"id":"quakes-3","section":"quakes","cat":"record","kind":"found","text":"The strongest earthquake ever measured struck southern Chile near Valdivia on 22 May 1960: magnitude 9.5. The shaking lasted about ten minutes. Hundreds of miles of coastline tore at once, and the tsunami it launched was still killing people in Hawaii and Japan a day later.","more":"In Hilo, Hawaii, the wave arrived about fifteen hours after the quake and killed 61 people. Japan is a quarter of the way around the world from Chile, and it got hit the following day."},{"id":"quakes-4","section":"quakes","cat":"speed","kind":"found","text":"In deep water a tsunami crosses the ocean at over 500 mph, about the speed of an airliner, while lifting the surface only a few feet. A ship sitting right on top of one feels nothing unusual. It only piles up into something terrible when it reaches shallow water.","more":"Buoys anchored in the deep ocean feel the wave pass underneath as a tiny change in sea level. They radio it to warning centers straight away. That is how a coast thousands of miles off gets hours of notice."},{"id":"quakes-5","section":"quakes","cat":"senses","kind":"found","text":"A tsunami announces itself. Sometimes the sea drains away first and leaves fish stranded. Sometimes it comes in and in and does not go out, frothing oddly. Tilly Smith was ten years old and standing at Mai Khao Beach in Thailand in 2004. She knew the water looked wrong, from a geography lesson two weeks earlier.","more":"She told her parents. Her father told a hotel security guard. The beach was cleared onto the second floor of a hotel before the wave arrived. Mai Khao was one of very few beaches on Phuket where nobody was killed."},{"id":"quakes-6","section":"quakes","cat":"speed","kind":"found","text":"The plates of Earth's surface drift about as fast as your fingernails grow. That is roughly one to four inches a year. The speed is measured, not reasoned out. GPS receivers are bolted to bedrock all over the world. They report their own positions, and those positions keep changing by exactly those small amounts.","more":"The Pacific plate is one of the quicker ones, moving something like 3 to 4 inches a year. The North American plate ambles along at closer to one inch."},{"id":"quakes-7","section":"quakes","cat":"build","kind":"worked","text":"Nobody has ever been below the crust, and nobody has seen the core. What is down there is worked out from the way earthquake waves bend, slow and stop. Sideways shear waves cannot travel through liquid, and they vanish partway down, so the outer core is reasoned to be molten.","more":"In 1936 the Danish seismologist Inge Lehmann noticed waves arriving where a fully liquid core should have left silence. She argued there was a solid inner core inside the liquid one. That is still how it is done. No camera, no sample, just wiggles on a chart."},{"id":"quakes-8","section":"quakes","cat":"size","kind":"found","text":"The deepest hole anyone has drilled is the Kola borehole in Russia, finished in 1989 at 40,230 feet (12,262 m). That is 7.6 miles straight down. The rock at the bottom was around 356 degrees F (180 C), far hotter than planned, and the drilling stopped.","more":"It never came close to leaving the crust. How much further the crust goes is not something anyone has dug to find out; it is worked out from earthquake waves."},{"id":"quakes-9","section":"quakes","cat":"size","kind":"found","text":"Big earthquakes are rarer than the news makes them feel. Take the average across the years 2000 to 2021. The world got about 135 quakes of magnitude 6 a year. It got around 13 of magnitude 7. It got roughly one of magnitude 8 or stronger. Small ones happen constantly, everywhere.","more":"Thousands of quakes too small to feel are recorded every single day. Most of what a seismograph draws is a planet quietly fidgeting."},{"id":"rocks-1","section":"rocks","cat":"record","kind":"found","text":"A mineral's hardness is really just a list of what it can scratch. Talc, number 1, gives way to a fingernail. Quartz, number 7, cuts glass. Diamond sits at 10, and the only thing that reliably scratches a diamond is another diamond.","more":"The ten steps in order are talc, gypsum, calcite, fluorite, apatite, orthoclase, quartz, topaz, corundum, diamond. Nothing on the list can scratch anything above it."},{"id":"rocks-10","section":"rocks","cat":"travel","kind":"found","text":"Pumice is rock full of frozen gas bubbles, and it floats. After some eruptions, whole rafts of it drift away across the open sea. Ships and satellites track those rafts for months. In the end the pumice waterlogs and sinks.","more":"Drop a piece in a glass and it bobs. Leave it long enough and water works into the holes and it goes under, which is how the rafts eventually break up."},{"id":"rocks-11","section":"rocks","cat":"senses","kind":"found","text":"Granite is not one substance. Look closely at a polished granite counter. The speckles are separate minerals grown together. There is glassy gray quartz, and blocky pink or white feldspar. The black flecks are mica, and they peel off in shiny sheets. You are looking at a mixture, not a material.","more":"Put your eye close to a granite countertop, step or gravestone. You can usually pick out three or four different kinds of grain with no magnifier at all."},{"id":"rocks-12","section":"rocks","cat":"created","kind":"record","text":"Genesis names minerals early. Describing the land of Havilah in chapter 2, it lists gold, bdellium and onyx stone. Onyx is banded quartz, and people still cut and polish it."},{"id":"rocks-2","section":"rocks","cat":"tryit","kind":"found","text":"Your fingernail is about 2.5 on the hardness scale and a copper coin is about 3. A rock your nail marks is softer than calcite. A rock that shrugs off a steel key is harder than about 5.5. That is the whole test geologists carry into the field.","tryit":"Pick up a rock outside. Drag a fingernail across it, then the edge of a copper coin, then a steel key. Whichever leaves a scratch tells you roughly how hard the rock is."},{"id":"rocks-3","section":"rocks","cat":"weird","kind":"found","text":"The hardness scale looks like a ruler and is not one. Corundum is 9 and diamond is 10, one step apart. But pressed with a hard point and measured properly, diamond resists about five times better than corundum. The numbers are an order of finish, not a measurement.","more":"On the Vickers test corundum comes in near 2,035 and diamond near 10,000. The gaps between the ten steps are all different sizes, and the last one is enormous."},{"id":"rocks-4","section":"rocks","cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"The hardness scale is named for Friedrich Mohs, a German mineral expert. In 1812 he lined ten minerals up in the order of which scratched which. He never measured a force or built a machine. He just made a ranking, and it was useful enough that it is still in every geology kit.","more":"This is history rather than a measurement. One man sorted a drawer of samples and wrote the order down. That is why the scale exists."},{"id":"rocks-5","section":"rocks","cat":"size","kind":"found","text":"Miners at Naica in Mexico broke into a chamber packed with clear gypsum beams. The largest measure about 37 feet (11 m) long and 13 feet (4 m) thick, and weigh roughly 55 tons. They are single crystals.","more":"The chamber sits near 136 degrees F (58 C). The air in it is almost completely saturated with water. Even in cooled suits, people could only stay a few minutes at a time. The mine's pumps were switched off in October 2015. The water came back and covered the crystals again."},{"id":"rocks-6","section":"rocks","cat":"weird","kind":"found","text":"Ruby and sapphire are the same mineral. Both are corundum, aluminum and oxygen. A trace of chromium turns it red and it gets called ruby. Traces of iron and titanium turn it blue and it gets called sapphire. Same hardness, same recipe, different pinch of impurity, wildly different price."},{"id":"rocks-7","section":"rocks","cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"Obsidian is glass a volcano made. Lava cooled so fast the minerals never got to line up into crystals. With no grain to follow, a flake of obsidian can come to an edge a few nanometers across, thinner than the edge of a steel razor. People chipped it into knives and arrowheads.","more":"A steel scalpel is blunt next to it. Obsidian blades have been used in some surgery, and in laboratory work where a very clean cut matters."},{"id":"rocks-8","section":"rocks","cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"Weigh the rock of Earth's crust and 46 percent of it is oxygen, the same element you are breathing, locked into minerals instead of floating loose. Silicon is another 28 percent. Most rock is those two joined together, with some aluminum and iron mixed in.","more":"Aluminum comes third at about 8 percent. Put oxygen, silicon and aluminum together and you have more than four fifths of everything you walk on."},{"id":"rocks-9","section":"rocks","cat":"home","kind":"found","text":"Table salt is a mineral. Its proper name is halite, and it is the one rock people eat on purpose. Halite always breaks along the same three directions, so the grains come apart into little cubes. Tip some onto dark paper and look closely and the cubes are right there.","more":"Halite is soft, only about 2 or 2.5 on the hardness scale, which is why salt crushes under a spoon and quartz does not."},{"id":"sky-1","section":"sky","cat":"weird","kind":"found","text":"The moon does spin. It just spins at exactly the rate it travels round us, one turn per orbit, about 27 days, so the same face stays pointed at earth the whole time. If the moon stopped spinning tomorrow, we would start seeing every side of it.","more":"Its orbit is slightly lopsided and slightly tilted, so from here the moon appears to rock and nod a little. Over a month that rocking lets us peek round the edges, and about 59 percent of the surface has now been seen from the ground.","tryit":"Find the moon in daylight. Around first quarter, when it is half lit, look up in the afternoon. It is high in the southern sky and pale blue-white against the blue. The moon is up in daytime about as often as at night. It just loses to the sun."},{"id":"sky-10","section":"sky","cat":"senses","kind":"worked","text":"Auroras are not reflections off polar ice, which is the old guess. Particles streaming off the sun get steered by earth's magnetic field down toward the poles, crash into oxygen and nitrogen high in the atmosphere and make them glow. It is a neon sign, using the whole sky as the tube.","more":"Nobody can see the particles. The cause is worked out rather than watched. Satellites measure the solar wind before it arrives. Lab tests show which gas glows what color. Oxygen gives green from roughly 60 to 120 miles (100 to 200 km) up, and red above that. The southern version is called the aurora australis. It often lights up at the same moment, in a matching shape."},{"id":"sky-11","section":"sky","cat":"size","kind":"found","text":"Most shooting stars are made by something between a grain of sand and a pea. It is moving so fast that the air piling up in front of it is crushed and heated until the grain glows white and is gone. About 48 tons of that dust hits the atmosphere every single day.","more":"The grain is not burning up from rubbing against the air, which is the usual explanation and is wrong. It is the air itself getting compressed and superheated in front of it, the same way a bicycle pump gets hot."},{"id":"sky-12","section":"sky","cat":"created","kind":"record","text":"Genesis day four: sun, moon and stars set in place for signs and for seasons, and for days and years. Read that way, the sky is a calendar. The spin that makes a day, the moon's cycle that makes a month, the 23.4-degree tilt that makes a year turn.","more":"Every piece of that calendar can be measured, and every piece has been. A turn takes 23 hours 56 minutes. One full moon to the next is about 29 and a half days. The tilt is 23.4 degrees. The Genesis account adds one thing to the numbers. They were put there on purpose, to be read."},{"id":"sky-2","section":"sky","cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"Nobody saw the far side of the moon until October 1959, when a Soviet probe called Luna 3 flew past and sent back photographs. Calling it the dark side is wrong: it gets as much sunlight as the side we look at. It is the hidden side, not the dark one.","more":"There were only a handful of pictures and they were grainy. But they showed something nobody expected. The far side is covered in craters. It has almost none of the big dark smooth plains that make the near side look like a face."},{"id":"sky-3","section":"sky","cat":"size","kind":"found","text":"Tides are measured with tide gauges, every day, everywhere. The biggest are in the Bay of Fundy in Canada, where the difference between low and high water averages about 52 feet (16 m). That is a four-story building. The worldwide average is closer to 3 feet (1 m).","more":"Roughly 110 billion tons (100 billion tonnes) of water moves in and out of that bay on one tide. That is more than every river on earth is carrying at that moment, put together. Boats in Fundy harbors sit flat on the mud twice a day."},{"id":"sky-4","section":"sky","cat":"build","kind":"worked","text":"Most coasts get two high tides a day, not one, and the reason is reasoned out rather than watched. The moon's pull heaps the ocean up on the side facing it, and also on the far side, where the water is pulled least and gets left behind. Earth turns through both bulges daily.","more":"The sun does the same thing, with about half the pull. When sun and moon line up you get the biggest tides of the month, called spring tides, which has nothing to do with the season. When they pull at right angles you get the smallest, called neap tides."},{"id":"sky-5","section":"sky","cat":"weird","kind":"found","text":"Seasons have nothing to do with how far away the sun is. Earth is actually closest to the sun in early January, in the dead middle of northern winter. What makes summer is tilt: earth's axis leans over by 23.4 degrees, so for half the year your side leans sunward.","more":"The gap between nearest and farthest is only about three percent. That is far too small to matter. There is a simpler way to kill the distance idea. Australia has summer while Canada has winter. Same planet, same distance, same day. Leaning toward the sun makes the light hit at a steeper angle. It gets squeezed onto less ground. The days are longer too."},{"id":"sky-6","section":"sky","cat":"tryit","kind":"found","text":"Your shadow is the hand of a clock. It swings around the ground all day because the earth is turning underneath you, at about 1,000 mph (1,600 km/h) if you are near the equator. You do not feel any of it, for the same reason you cannot feel the speed of a smooth airplane.","more":"The shadow also changes length through the year. Shortest at midday in summer, when the sun is high, longest at midday in winter, when it is low. That change is earth's 23.4-degree tilt, showing up on your driveway.","tryit":"Stand somewhere sunny and have someone put a stone at the tip of your shadow. Go away for fifteen minutes. Come back, stand in exactly the same footprints, and put down a second stone. The gap between the stones is the earth turning."},{"id":"sky-7","section":"sky","cat":"weird","kind":"found","text":"Earth turns once in 23 hours 56 minutes and 4 seconds, measured against the stars. Our day is 24 hours because we time it by the sun instead. Earth slides along its orbit while it spins. So it has to turn a little extra to face the sun again.","more":"Those four missing minutes a day add up. The stars come back around four minutes earlier each night. That is two hours a month. It is why the night sky in January is not the night sky in July."},{"id":"sky-8","section":"sky","cat":"speed","kind":"found","text":"Sunlight takes about 8 minutes and 20 seconds to get here, crossing roughly 93 million miles (150 million km). So the sun you can see out of the window is the sun as it was a few minutes ago. Everything in the sky is a picture of the past, just by a little.","more":"Light from the moon takes about 1.3 seconds, which is why radio conversations with Apollo crews had that awkward pause in them. Light from the nearest star after the sun takes over four years."},{"id":"sky-9","section":"sky","cat":"weird","kind":"found","text":"The sun is about 400 times wider than the moon. It also sits about 400 times further away. Those two numbers cancel out. So the sun and the moon look almost exactly the same size in our sky. It is why the moon can cover the sun during a total eclipse.","more":"No other planet with moons gets a fit that neat. The fit is not permanent either. The moon drifts about an inch and a half further from earth each year. One day it will look too small to cover the sun completely."},{"id":"ice-1","section":"ice","cat":"size","kind":"found","text":"About nine-tenths of an iceberg is underwater. For the same amount of space, ice is roughly nine-tenths as heavy as seawater. So a berg sinks until it has shoved aside its own weight in water. Only that last tenth sticks up where you can see it.","more":"This is why a berg can roll over without warning. As the underwater part melts unevenly, the whole thing becomes top-heavy and flips, and there is no way to see it coming from a ship. Captains keep well clear of the sides, not just the front."},{"id":"ice-10","section":"ice","cat":"tryit","kind":"found","text":"Your breath is full of water vapor you cannot see. Push it against something colder than the air and the vapor turns straight back into liquid: fog on the glass. If the glass is below freezing, it skips being liquid altogether and goes directly to ice crystals, which is frost.","more":"The same thing is happening on the inside of a cold window in winter, in the mist that comes off a cold drink can, and in a cloud. In all four cases warm air holding invisible water meets something cold and has to let the water go.","tryit":"Breathe hard on a cold window and watch the patch appear, then shrink and vanish. On a freezing morning, do it on the outside of a car window instead and get close: the fog arrives as tiny crystals, not as droplets."},{"id":"ice-11","section":"ice","cat":"weird","kind":"found","text":"At the South Pole the sun rises once a year and sets once a year. It comes up around late September, then circles round and round the sky without setting for six months, and goes down around late March. One day and one night, six months each.","more":"It does not go straight up and straight down there either. It spirals: a slow climb to about 23 degrees above the horizon at midsummer, then a slow spiral back down. Sunrise at the Pole takes days."},{"id":"ice-12","section":"ice","cat":"created","kind":"record","text":"Genesis day one: light, and light divided from darkness, and the two of them named Day and Night. All of that before the sun is made on day four. At the South Pole, Day and Night are six months long apiece. This is the written account, not something anyone measured.","more":"Standing at the Pole is the one place on earth where day and night are not tied to the sun coming up in the morning. They are a single long turn of light and dark, which is a strange thing to stand inside of."},{"id":"ice-2","section":"ice","cat":"size","kind":"found","text":"At the South Pole the ice is about 9,000 feet (2,700 m) thick. Nearly two miles of it. The rock underneath is close to sea level. So almost all of the Pole's 9,301-foot (2,835 m) elevation is frozen water standing on the ground. The research station stands on top of the water.","more":"The depth is read by flying radar over the ice and timing how long the echo takes to come back off the rock below. Where that has been checked against actual drilled holes, it lines up."},{"id":"ice-3","section":"ice","cat":"travel","kind":"found","text":"The marker at the geographic South Pole is dug up and moved every New Year's Day. The ice it stands on, along with the station built on that ice, drifts about 33 feet (10 m) a year toward the Weddell Sea. Leave it alone and it stops marking the pole.","more":"The crew who winter over design and machine a new brass marker each year, and the old one is retired. It may be the only signpost on earth that has to keep chasing the place it names."},{"id":"ice-4","section":"ice","cat":"size","kind":"found","text":"The thickest ice measured anywhere in Antarctica is close to 3 miles (4.9 km) deep. It sits in the Astrolabe Subglacial Basin. Across the whole continent the average is about 1.4 miles (2.2 km). Ice covers roughly 98 percent of Antarctica. Most maps of it are really maps of the lid.","more":"There are mountain ranges under there that no one has ever seen, mapped only by radar. There are lakes too, kept liquid by the pressure and the warmth of the rock, sitting under miles of ice."},{"id":"ice-5","section":"ice","cat":"size","kind":"worked","text":"If every bit of Antarctica's ice melted, sea level would go up about 190 feet (58 m). Greenland's would add roughly 24 feet (7.4 m). Nobody has measured that, obviously. Those are sums, worked out from the measured volume of the ice and the measured area of the world's oceans.","more":"Two numbers, two different kinds of claim, in one sentence. The volume of the ice is surveyed. The sea level figure is arithmetic done on that survey. Neither one is a prediction about whether it will happen."},{"id":"ice-6","section":"ice","cat":"weird","kind":"found","text":"Floating sea ice melting does not raise sea level. It is already shoving aside exactly its own weight in water, so turning back into water changes nothing. Ice sitting on land is a completely different case, because that water is not in the ocean yet. Same substance, opposite result.","more":"Float an ice cube in a glass filled to the very brim and mark the level. When it melts, the glass still does not overflow. Now set a second cube on a saucer, let it melt, and pour that in. That one is land ice."},{"id":"ice-7","section":"ice","cat":"senses","kind":"found","text":"Snow is white and glacier ice is blue, and both are made of the identical clear stuff. Snow is packed with tiny surfaces that bounce every color of light straight back out at you. Thick solid ice lets light travel far enough inside to soak up the reds, and blue is what comes back.","more":"You can watch the effect switch on. A shallow scoop of snow is white. Punch a deep hole into a glacier and the bottom goes blue. Down there the light has to travel further through solid ice to reach your eye."},{"id":"ice-8","section":"ice","cat":"speed","kind":"found","text":"Jakobshavn Glacier in Greenland moves more than 150 feet (45 m) a day in summer. That is about an inch and a half every minute, which means a stake driven into the ice shifts several feet while you eat lunch. It is among the fastest-moving glaciers measured anywhere.","more":"It moves that fast because meltwater gets down to the bottom and the whole slab slides on it, rather than just creeping. Most glaciers are far lazier, moving a few inches a day, which is where the insult glacial pace came from."},{"id":"ice-9","section":"ice","cat":"home","kind":"found","text":"Permafrost is not ice. It is ground, any ground, that has stayed at or below 32 F (0 C) for at least two years running. About a quarter of the land in the northern half of the world qualifies, and in places it reaches down more than 4,900 feet (1,500 m).","more":"The top layer thaws every summer and refreezes every winter. Trees, moss and mosquitoes live on a thin lid over frozen ground. Put a heated house straight on that lid and the heat thaws it. The house sinks. That is why such houses are lifted off the ground first."},{"id":"volcanoes-1","section":"volcanoes","cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"Magma and lava are the same melted rock with different addresses. Underground it is magma. The second it comes out of the vent it is lava. Geologists are strict about this, and it is the cheapest way there is to sound like you know what you are talking about."},{"id":"volcanoes-10","section":"volcanoes","cat":"size","kind":"found","text":"Measured from the sea floor it stands on, the Hawaiian volcano Mauna Kea rises about 30,610 feet (9,330 m). Everest is 29,032 feet (8,849 m) above sea level. Mauna Kea is the taller mountain and loses the title on a technicality, because most of it is underwater.","more":"Only 13,803 feet (4,207 m) of Mauna Kea sticks out above the sea. The rest is a slope you would have to dive to see."},{"id":"volcanoes-11","section":"volcanoes","cat":"created","kind":"record","text":"Genesis 7 does not describe the Flood as rain alone. It says the fountains of the great deep were broken up, and then that the windows of heaven were opened. Water out of the ground first, water out of the sky second. That is the order the written account gives.","more":"A geyser and a spring both send water bursting upward out of the ground. The Genesis account puts that before the rain rather than after it."},{"id":"volcanoes-12","section":"volcanoes","cat":"speed","kind":"found","text":"Films get this backwards. Most lava flows creep along slower than you walk, and people stand next to them taking photographs. The killer is the pyroclastic flow: a ground-hugging cloud of hot ash and gas that comes down the slope faster than 50 mph (80 km/h).","more":"That is what reached Pompeii and Herculaneum. Volcanologists worry less about where the lava is going. They watch whether the eruption column is about to collapse."},{"id":"volcanoes-2","section":"volcanoes","cat":"record","kind":"found","text":"Lava at Kilauea in Hawaii comes out around 2,140 degrees F (1,170 C), and inside a lava tube it can hold near 2,200 degrees F (1,200 C). That is not an estimate. Geologists walk up to the flow and push a metal probe called a thermocouple straight into it.","more":"Some flows cannot be safely reached. They point calibrated thermal cameras at those instead. Then they check the camera readings against the ones taken with the probe."},{"id":"volcanoes-3","section":"volcanoes","cat":"weird","kind":"found","text":"Ol Doinyo Lengai in Tanzania erupts the coolest lava on Earth, about 930 to 1,110 degrees F (500 to 600 C). It is cool enough that it does not glow in daylight. It pours out looking like runny black oil, and rain turns the hardened flows chalk white.","more":"Ordinary lava is made of silicate minerals. This lava is made of sodium and potassium carbonates instead. That is why it is so runny and so cool. It also turns white when water gets at it."},{"id":"volcanoes-4","section":"volcanoes","cat":"size","kind":"found","text":"About 1,350 volcanoes on land are counted as capable of erupting. That does not include the near-continuous chain of them running along the sea floor. About 500 of the land ones have actually erupted within written history. On a typical day, roughly 20 are erupting somewhere in the world.","more":"About 170 of those volcanoes sit in the United States and its territories. That is why the USGS keeps watch on them. It runs volcano observatories in Hawaii, Alaska, California and Yellowstone."},{"id":"volcanoes-5","section":"volcanoes","cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"The eruption that buried Pompeii in AD 79 has an eyewitness. Pliny the Younger watched it from across the bay. He later wrote two letters about it to the historian Tacitus. He described a cloud shaped like an umbrella pine. A tall trunk, with branches spreading at the top.","more":"His description was good enough that volcanologists named that kind of eruption after him. A tall column punching straight up into the sky is still called Plinian."},{"id":"volcanoes-6","section":"volcanoes","cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"Krakatoa exploded on the morning of 27 August 1883. People on Rodrigues Island heard what they wrote down as the distant roar of heavy guns. That island sits 3,000 miles (4,800 km) away in the Indian Ocean. Perth, Australia, is 1,930 miles (3,110 km) off, and they heard it there too.","more":"Barographs, instruments that scratch a line of air pressure onto a paper drum, recorded the pressure wave passing seven times over five days. It went out to the far side of the world and back, out and back, until it faded. Nobody at those weather stations knew yet what had happened."},{"id":"volcanoes-7","section":"volcanoes","cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"Tambora in Indonesia erupted in April 1815 and blew about 4,200 feet off its own summit. The next year, farmers in New England wrote down frost in every single month and a snowstorm in June. They called 1816 the year without a summer, and also Eighteen Hundred and Froze to Death.","more":"On 5 June 1816 the temperature in Ipswich, Massachusetts hit 92 degrees F. By the next morning it had fallen to 43. Crops failed across the northeast and in Europe, and people who kept diaries recorded all of it as it happened."},{"id":"volcanoes-8","section":"volcanoes","cat":"build","kind":"worked","text":"Nobody has ever looked into a magma chamber. Their size and depth are worked out, not seen. Earthquake waves slow down when they pass through melted rock. The ground above swells by a few inches before an eruption. Instruments read both. Eyes read neither.","more":"The tilt is far too small to notice standing on it. It is measured with tiltmeters and with satellites that compare radar images of the same hillside taken weeks apart."},{"id":"volcanoes-9","section":"volcanoes","cat":"speed","kind":"found","text":"Old Faithful in Yellowstone throws boiling water 106 to 184 feet (30 to 55 m) into the air about every 90 minutes. Rangers predict it by timing the eruption before: the longer it plays, the longer the wait for the next one. Each eruption empties 3,700 to 8,400 gallons.","more":"It is neither the biggest geyser in the park nor the most punctual. It is simply the big one that keeps to a schedule people can plan a day around."},{"id":"water-1","section":"water","cat":"size","kind":"found","text":"The deepest measured spot in the ocean is the Challenger Deep. It lies at the southern end of the Mariana Trench, 35,876 feet (10,935 m) below the surface. Drop Mount Everest into it and more than a mile of water would still cover the summit.","more":"That figure comes from a careful 2020 sonar survey and is good to about 20 feet either way. It is a depth someone measured, not a depth anyone calculated."},{"id":"water-10","section":"water","cat":"tryit","kind":"found","text":"A cold glass does not leak. The water on the outside came out of the air in the room. Air carries invisible water, and chilling it makes the air let go. That single step, warm damp air meeting something cold, is how clouds and rain get made.","tryit":"Fill a glass with ice water and leave it a few minutes. Wipe the outside completely dry with your sleeve, then watch it go wet again. Nothing came through the glass. The room handed it over."},{"id":"water-11","section":"water","cat":"build","kind":"worked","text":"The depth of a trench is measured. How it got there is not. Nobody has watched a trench form. The deepest ones are worked out to be places where one slab of sea floor bends under another. The reason is the earthquakes below them. They line up on a slanting sheet that runs down and away.","more":"The measured part is where each quake happened and how deep. The bending slab is the explanation that fits the pattern, and an explanation is a different kind of thing from a depth reading."},{"id":"water-12","section":"water","cat":"created","kind":"record","text":"In Genesis, water is there before anything has a shape. On day two the waters are divided, water below from water above. On day three the water below is gathered into one place so that dry land can appear. The written account makes water the setting everything else gets arranged in.","more":"This one is a record, not a reading. The ocean really is one connected body of water, not several. That is why a bottle dropped off Japan can wash up in California."},{"id":"water-2","section":"water","cat":"weird","kind":"found","text":"At the bottom of the Challenger Deep the water presses in at over a thousand times the pressure at the surface, roughly eight tons on every square inch. Anything with air inside it, including a person, would be crushed flat. People have still been down there, inside thick steel spheres.","more":"Jacques Piccard and Don Walsh reached the bottom in 1960. James Cameron went alone in 2012, and Victor Vescovo has been down repeatedly since 2019."},{"id":"water-3","section":"water","cat":"size","kind":"found","text":"Of all the water on Earth, 96.5 percent is salt water sitting in the oceans. Of the small slice that is fresh, most is not available at all: nearly 69 percent of it is locked up as ice, and about 30 percent is underground. Lakes and rivers get the leftovers.","more":"Add up every lake, river, swamp and puddle. Together they come to well under one percent of the world's fresh water. And fresh water is under three percent of all the water there is."},{"id":"water-4","section":"water","cat":"weird","kind":"found","text":"Every river on Earth put together holds about one ten-thousandth of one percent of the planet's water. Nearly all the water people drink, wash in and grow food with comes from that sliver. It only works because rain keeps refilling it faster than we empty it.","more":"A river is less a store of water than a queue of it. What is in the Mississippi today is not what was in it last month."},{"id":"water-5","section":"water","cat":"size","kind":"found","text":"The Amazon pours about 7.6 million cubic feet of fresh water into the Atlantic every second. That is five times the Congo and twelve times the Mississippi. Roughly one fifth of all the river water reaching the sea comes out of this one river.","more":"So much comes out that the sea off the mouth is noticeably less salty. Sailors used to refill their water barrels there, still out of sight of land."},{"id":"water-6","section":"water","cat":"record","kind":"found","text":"Tides in the Bay of Fundy in Canada rise and fall about 52 feet (16 m) twice a day. Boats afloat at breakfast sit on mud by lunch. Around 100 billion tons of water move in and out each cycle, more than every river in the world carries in that same stretch of time.","more":"The highest level ever recorded there was 71 feet (21.6 m), in October 1869. A storm surge happened to land on top of an already big tide."},{"id":"water-7","section":"water","cat":"travel","kind":"found","text":"High tide arrives about 50 minutes later each day. So does the moon. That is no coincidence, and nobody has to take it on trust. Tide tables are printed a year in advance. Boats stake their keels on those tables being right.","more":"The moon takes 24 hours and 50 minutes to come back to the same place in your sky. The whole tidal pattern slides later at exactly that rate."},{"id":"water-8","section":"water","cat":"weird","kind":"found","text":"The ocean covers about 70 percent of the planet, and only around 29 percent of the sea floor has been mapped in detail with modern sonar. The moon has been mapped in finer detail than the bottom of our own sea.","more":"Mapping is not the same as seeing. A camera or a person has looked at less than one thousandth of one percent of the deep sea floor. That is an area about the size of Rhode Island."},{"id":"water-9","section":"water","cat":"size","kind":"found","text":"Angel Falls in Venezuela drops 3,212 feet (979 m) in total. One unbroken plunge accounts for 2,648 feet (807 m) of that. The water falls for a long time before anything interrupts it. A good deal of it arrives at the bottom as drifting spray rather than a river.","more":"Jimmie Angel was an American pilot. He flew over the falls on 16 November 1933, and the falls are named for him. The name first appeared on a Venezuelan government map in December 1939."},{"id":"caves-1","section":"caves","cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"On 19 March 1982 a small eruption melted the snow in Mount St. Helens' crater. Close to a billion gallons (4 million cubic meters) of water burst out and tore down the North Fork Toutle River valley, cutting as it went. Nobody watched the Grand Canyon get cut. People watched this one.","more":"Geologists had surveyed marked lines across the channel weeks before. They surveyed the same lines again on 20 March, the morning after. Ten miles (16 km) below the crater the riverbed had dropped an average of 9 ft (2.8 m) overnight. Downstream, four men stood at a gauging station and sampled the flood as it went past. The stuff it cut through was loose volcanic debris dumped by the 1980 eruption, not hard rock, which is a fair part of why it went so fast."},{"id":"caves-10","section":"caves","cat":"home","kind":"found","text":"Movile Cave in Romania was found in 1986 by workers digging on a building site near the Black Sea. It is sealed off from the surface. Fifty-seven animal species have been counted living in it, thirty-seven of them found nowhere else on earth, and most of them have no eyes at all.","more":"Nothing down there runs on sunlight. The bottom of the food chain is bacteria pulling energy out of hydrogen sulfide and methane in the water. That is the rotten-egg gas, and the air in the cave is dangerous to breathe, so scientists go in with tanks and stay under an hour."},{"id":"caves-11","section":"caves","cat":"weird","kind":"found","text":"On 8 May 1981 the ground opened up in Winter Park, Florida. Over two days the hole widened to 320 feet (98 m) across and 90 feet (27 m) deep. It swallowed a three-bedroom house, a huge sycamore tree, five Porsches from a repair shop, a pickup truck and the town swimming pool.","more":"Florida is built on limestone riddled with holes. This one let go during a stretch of record-low water in the aquifer underneath, when the water that had been helping hold the roof up was gone. The hole is now a lake, and the town keeps it as a park."},{"id":"caves-12","section":"caves","cat":"created","kind":"record","text":"Genesis says the fountains of the great deep burst open. It says the windows of heaven were opened, and water covered the whole earth. Stand on the Grand Canyon rim and look. You see flat stacked layers, with a canyon cut down through them. Laid down first, then carved.","more":"Note what is and is not being claimed. The layers are there and can be photographed. The canyon is a mile deep and can be measured. Which event laid the layers and cut the canyon is not something a tape measure can settle, so it is marked here as coming from the record."},{"id":"caves-13","section":"caves","cat":"size","kind":"found","text":"Hang Son Doong in Vietnam holds the largest cave passage ever measured: about 660 feet (200 m) high and 490 feet (150 m) wide, running for more than 3 miles (5 km). A Boeing 747 could fly down the middle of it without its wingtips touching either wall.","more":"Two stretches of its ceiling have collapsed, which lets daylight down into the cave. Trees grow inside, under those holes. On humid days a cloud forms indoors, near the roof, and it rains inside the cave."},{"id":"caves-2","section":"caves","cat":"travel","kind":"record","text":"A Vietnamese man named Ho Khanh found the entrance to Hang Son Doong, the largest cave passage ever measured, in 1990, while looking for agarwood to sell. Then he could not find it again. He relocated it in 2008. In April 2009 a team of British cavers surveyed it and worked out what he had walked into.","more":"The entrance is a hole in a jungle hillside with a cold draft coming out of it, and the roar of an underground river below. It is not obvious from outside that it opens into the biggest passage anyone has measured."},{"id":"caves-3","section":"caves","cat":"build","kind":"worked","text":"Nobody has ever watched a cave form. The explanation is worked out, not observed: rain picks up carbon dioxide from the air and the soil, becomes a weak acid, and eats limestone along cracks. You can check the middle of that argument, though. Water leaving cave springs is carrying dissolved limestone right now.","more":"The step that is genuinely measured is the chemistry. Put limestone in fizzy water and it dissolves. Test water coming out of a cave and it is loaded with calcium it did not have going in. The size of the hole is measured too. It is the story joining the two that is reasoning."},{"id":"caves-4","section":"caves","cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"Stalactites hang from the ceiling. Stalagmites stand on the ground. The letters do the remembering: C for ceiling, G for ground. They usually come as a matched pair, because one drip builds both. It leaves a smear of mineral on the ceiling on its way past. The rest of it lands below.","more":"The longest hanging stalactite you can walk up to and look at is about 27 feet (8.2 m) long. It is in the Jeita Grotto in Lebanon. A stalactite and the stalagmite under it sometimes meet. The join is called a column."},{"id":"caves-5","section":"caves","cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"A stalactite starts as a hollow tube a fifth of an inch (4 to 5 mm) across, with water running down the inside. Cavers call those soda straws. When the tube clogs, water starts running down the outside instead, and the fat cone shape begins. Average thickening: 0.005 inches (0.13 mm) a year.","more":"That is the average. A stalactite under a fast steady drip can add ten times as much. Concrete under a bridge can grow one at 2 mm a day. Growth depends on the drip, not on the calendar."},{"id":"caves-6","section":"caves","cat":"size","kind":"found","text":"Mammoth Cave in Kentucky is the longest cave known. It holds 426 miles (686 km) of mapped passage. No other cave on earth comes close. That figure is not an estimate. Volunteers from the Cave Research Foundation crawled it with tape measures and compasses, survey station by survey station.","more":"Before the Flint Ridge system was connected to it, Mammoth was a 44-mile cave. The extra 380-odd miles came from people going back again and again to check whether a crack was wide enough to squeeze through."},{"id":"caves-7","section":"caves","cat":"record","kind":"found","text":"The deepest known cave is Veryovkina, in the Arabika massif in Georgia. Its floor sits 7,257 feet (2,212 m) below its entrance, which is well over a mile straight down. When it was first written up in 1968, it was thought to be only 377 feet (115 m) deep.","more":"The entrance is a hole about 10 feet by 13 feet in a mountainside. Reaching the bottom takes days, and expeditions have been chased back out by floodwater rising up the shafts."},{"id":"caves-8","section":"caves","cat":"size","kind":"found","text":"The Grand Canyon is measured, not guessed at. It runs 278 river miles (447 km) long. It is up to 18 miles (29 km) from rim to rim. From rim to river is about one mile (1.6 km) straight down. The South Rim stands near 7,000 feet. The North Rim stands near 8,000.","more":"The two rims are about 10 miles apart on average, but roughly 215 miles apart by road, because you have to drive all the way around. Hiking rim to rim is about 21 miles and is not a day trip for most people."},{"id":"caves-9","section":"caves","cat":"weird","kind":"worked","text":"How deep the Grand Canyon is and how it was cut are two different kinds of statement. The depth is measured with instruments. The cutting was never watched. That account is worked backwards from the shape of the rock, the bends in the river and the gravel it dropped. Geologists still disagree about it.","more":"This is the honest position, and it is not a dodge. A canyon does not come with a label. Everything anyone says about how it got there is reasoning from what is left behind, and reasoning can be revised. The tape measure cannot."}];
globalThis.EARTH = EARTH;
